# Entity Layer Architecture

Two entity types, in separate projects:

| Layer | Project | Purpose |
|-------|---------|---------|
| Domain entities | `NTierTemplate/` | Shared types, message contracts, entities with focused behavior |
| EF entities | `NTierTemplate.Data/` | Persistence models mapping to tables/columns; used only in DAOs and DbContext |

## Domain Project

Domain holds **shared types and message contracts** — not use-case orchestration. Behavior belongs on domain entities only when it is small and fits comfortably there. Everything else lives in Application services.

## Rules

- **Application services** (`UserService`, etc.) live in `NTierTemplate.Application/`; they use domain types and DAO interfaces only
- When investigating database issues or schema: look at EF entities in `NTierTemplate.Data/`, not domain entities
- When writing LINQ queries or EF Core code: reference EF entities in `NTierTemplate.Data/`
- DAOs translate between EF entities and domain entities at CRUD and query level
- ASP.NET Core Identity persistence types (`ApplicationUser`, `ApplicationRole`) live in `NTierTemplate.Data/`; map to domain `User` in DAOs
- Identity managers (`UserManager`, `RoleManager`) are used inside DAO implementations in `NTierTemplate.Data/`, not in Application services, Api controllers, Cli handlers, or Queue consumers
- Api, Cli, and Queue resolve Application service interfaces (`IUserService`, etc.), not DAO interfaces or Identity types

